  "worked_solution": "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{\u23df}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
